  "summary": "Toledo City Council's Public Safety Committee debated a one-year extension of the city's Flock ALPR contract, with police and prosecutors backing the technology and most public commenters opposing it over privacy concerns. Council members questioned Flock about data sharing, retention, and misuse, and police said Toledo has enabled filters blocking immigration and reproductive-health searches. A vote is expected Tuesday after a two-year extension was defeated the prior week.",
  "key_quotes": [
    {
      "quote": "Just about every felony case has used Flock in one way or another",
      "paragraph_idx": 6
    },
    {
      "quote": "Every city has control over what they share and who they share it with",
      "paragraph_idx": 10
    },
    {
      "quote": "Once that data is deleted, it is hard deleted off of all of the servers",
      "paragraph_idx": 11
    },
    {
      "quote": "Toledo has enabled filters that block both immigration and reproductive health searches",
      "paragraph_idx": 12
    },
    {
      "quote": "Council is expected to vote on the contract Tuesday, following the defeat of a two-year extension proposal last week.",
      "paragraph_idx": 20
    }
  ],
